  window.BIBLE_ENTRIES.push.apply(window.BIBLE_ENTRIES, [{"day": 31, "name": "Sarah", "book": "Genesis 17–23", "answer": "Sarah", "facts": ["I had my first and only child at the age of 90!", "My name means “princess.”", "My son Isaac's name literally means “he laughs,” because I laughed when I heard I'd have a baby."], "story": "Sarah waited decades for God's promise that she and Abraham would have a child of their own. It seemed impossible — she was far too old to be a mother by the time it happened. When messengers told her she'd finally have a baby, she laughed in disbelief. But sure enough, Isaac was born, and Sarah's long wait became one of the Bible's clearest examples that God can do things that seem completely impossible.", "color": "#d99a3a", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 32, "name": "Jonathan", "book": "1 Samuel 14, 18–20, 23, 31", "answer": "Jonathan", "facts": ["I once climbed a cliff and attacked an entire Philistine outpost with just one other soldier helping me.", "I gave David my royal robe and weapons as a sign of our deep friendship.", "The Bible says our souls were “knit together” — we were incredibly close friends."], "story": "Jonathan was King Saul's brave son and heir to the throne, but he's remembered most for his extraordinary friendship with David. Even though David's rising popularity could have threatened Jonathan's own claim to become king, Jonathan chose loyalty and love over jealousy, protecting David from his father's anger on multiple occasions. Their friendship remains one of the most celebrated examples of true loyalty in the Bible.", "color": "#c9622c", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 33, "name": "Jeroboam", "book": "1 Kings 11–14", "answer": "Jeroboam", "facts": ["A prophet gave me ten torn pieces of his cloak, symbolizing ten tribes that would follow me.", "I became the first king of the northern kingdom after it split from Judah.", "I set up golden calf idols, a decision the Bible remembers as a serious mistake."], "story": "Jeroboam was told by a prophet that he would one day rule over ten of Israel's twelve tribes, and that prophecy came true after King Rehoboam's harsh decisions caused the kingdom to split. Jeroboam became the first king of the new northern kingdom of Israel, but instead of trusting God to keep his throne secure, he made the serious mistake of setting up golden calf idols for the people to worship — a choice that had lasting negative consequences for the northern kingdom.", "color": "#b9862c", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 34, "name": "Obadiah", "book": "1 Kings 18", "answer": "Obadiah", "facts": ["I secretly hid 100 of God's prophets in two caves to save their lives.", "I served as the palace official in charge of King Ahab's household.", "I helped set up the famous meeting between Elijah and Ahab that led to the Mount Carmel showdown."], "story": "Obadiah worked as a high-ranking official in King Ahab's palace, even though he remained faithful to God during a time when many in the kingdom had turned away. At great personal risk, he secretly hid 100 of God's prophets in caves to protect them from Queen Jezebel's persecution. Obadiah's quiet courage, working faithfully even inside a corrupt system, makes him a lesser-known but meaningful example of standing firm in difficult circumstances.", "color": "#8c3a3a", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 35, "name": "Daniel", "book": "Daniel 1–12", "answer": "Daniel", "facts": ["I spent an entire night in a den of lions and wasn't harmed at all.", "I could interpret dreams that stumped Babylon's wisest advisors.", "I chose to eat only vegetables and water rather than the king's rich food, and I stayed healthy and strong."], "story": "Daniel was taken from his homeland as a young man to serve in the courts of Babylon, but he remained committed to honoring God even in a foreign land, starting with his choice to avoid the king's food. His God-given ability to interpret dreams and visions earned him great respect among Babylon's rulers. Even when jealous rivals tricked a king into having him thrown into a den of lions for praying, Daniel's faith never wavered — and God kept him completely safe through the night.", "color": "#3a7fd9", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 36, "name": "Eli", "book": "1 Samuel 1–4", "answer": "Eli", "facts": ["I served as both priest and judge over Israel for 40 years.", "I raised and trained the boy Samuel at the temple.", "I was the one who helped Samuel realize it was God calling his name at night."], "story": "Eli was the priest in charge of Israel's tabernacle when Hannah brought her young son Samuel to be raised in service to God. Eli took on the responsibility of training the boy, and it was Eli who eventually helped Samuel understand that the mysterious voice calling his name in the night was actually God speaking to him. Though Eli's own family faced serious struggles, his role in shaping young Samuel had a lasting impact on Israel's future.", "color": "#a89a7a", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 37, "name": "Othniel", "book": "Judges 1, 3", "answer": "Othniel", "facts": ["I was Israel's very first judge.", "I won my wife's hand in marriage by successfully capturing a city.", "My leadership brought forty years of peace to Israel."], "story": "Othniel became Israel's first judge during a time when the nation had fallen into a difficult cycle of turning away from God and facing hardship as a result. When the Israelites cried out for help, God raised up Othniel to lead them, and he successfully defeated an oppressive foreign king, bringing forty years of peace to the land. As the first of Israel's judges, Othniel set the pattern for the leaders who would follow him during this challenging era.", "color": "#c9a23a", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 38, "name": "Bathsheba", "book": "2 Samuel 11–12; 1 Kings 1–2", "answer": "Bathsheba", "facts": ["My son Solomon became one of the wisest and wealthiest kings in Israel's history.", "I later helped make sure Solomon, rather than another son, became king after David.", "I'm mentioned in the family tree of Jesus in the New Testament."], "story": "Bathsheba's story includes some of the most difficult chapters in King David's reign, involving painful choices and real loss, including the death of her first husband and her first child. Despite this hard beginning, Bathsheba became one of David's wives, and years later, she played an important role in making sure her son Solomon became the next king of Israel — a king remembered for his extraordinary wisdom.", "color": "#a83a5c", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 39, "name": "Malachi", "book": "Malachi 1–4", "answer": "Malachi", "facts": ["My book is the very last one in the Old Testament.", "I challenged the priests of my time for offering God their leftover, lower-quality sacrifices.", "My final prophecy about a coming messenger connects directly to John the Baptist in the New Testament."], "story": "Malachi was the final prophet of the Old Testament, delivering God's messages during a time when the people and even the priests had grown careless and half-hearted in their worship. He challenged them directly, calling for renewed sincerity and faithfulness. Malachi's book closes the Old Testament with a promise: that a messenger would be sent to prepare the way before a great day of the Lord — a prophecy that points forward to John the Baptist and the beginning of the New Testament story.", "color": "#8c7a5c", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}, {"day": 40, "name": "Isaac", "book": "Genesis 21–27", "answer": "Isaac", "facts": ["My name means “he laughs,” because my mother Sarah laughed when she heard I was coming.", "I was almost sacrificed on a mountain, but God provided a ram instead at the last second.", "My twin sons Jacob and Esau grew up to become very different men."], "story": "Isaac was the miracle son born to Abraham and Sarah in their old age, just as God had promised. As a young man he faced an intense test when his father was asked by God to offer him as a sacrifice — but God stopped Abraham and provided a ram instead, proving His faithfulness. Isaac grew up to marry Rebekah and became the father of twin sons, Jacob and Esau, continuing the family God had promised to bless.", "color": "#c9963a", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.75}]);
